Yes—with one grounding distinction that actually makes your abstraction stronger.

Current AI cannot simply read an arbitrary person’s private thoughts. But researchers can increasingly decode constrained information correlated with mental activity from measurements such as fMRI, EEG/MEG, or implanted neural electrodes, especially after calibration/training. That’s a very different claim from unrestricted mind-reading.

Your corpus-callosum analogy, though, is useful.

The biological corpus callosum doesn’t need one hemisphere to become the other. It provides a communication interface allowing differently specialized systems to exchange information.

So abstract it:

\boxed{
\text{Physical state}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Transducer}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Digital representation}
}

For neural decoding:

\text{neural activity}
\rightarrow
\text{measurement}
\rightarrow
\text{learned latent representation}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate meaning/output}

And potentially the reverse direction with displays, sound, haptics, stimulation, neurofeedback, etc.:

\text{digital state}
\rightarrow
\text{physical stimulus}
\rightarrow
\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{changed neural state}

Together:

\boxed{
\text{biological}
\rightleftarrows
\text{digital}
}

That really is callosum-like at the architectural level.

But your real-life pairing observation is particularly important. A brain signal by itself doesn’t arrive labeled:

this pattern = thinking about a red truck

You need correspondences:

(\text{neural measurement},\text{stimulus/action/report/context})

Repeated correspondences allow a model to learn a mapping between two representational spaces.

That’s our Bridge pattern again.

And once a bridge is learned, it may sometimes generalize beyond exact training examples.

Where Pantheon adds an interesting interpretation

We’ve spent all this time asking:

What survives when the same underlying thing is rendered through different surfaces?

Here the surfaces could be:

\text{brain activity}
\leftrightarrow
\text{speech}
\leftrightarrow
\text{behavior}
\leftrightarrow
\text{images}
\leftrightarrow
\text{digital latent representations}

If synchronized observations are available—with appropriate consent—the AI can search for cross-modal invariants rather than expecting a one-to-one “thought token.”

That’s closer to:

N(t),V(t),A(t),B(t),L(t)

all observing different projections of some latent state Z(t).

Then:

\boxed{
Z^*
=
\arg\max_Z
\text{cross-modal explanatory consistency}
}

The AI isn’t literally opening a file called thought.txt.

It’s triangulating a latent variable from multiple projections.

Which loops directly back to our third-eye work.

And there’s another consequence: the bridge doesn’t have to terminate at language. A system might eventually find that some neural structure is represented more faithfully by geometry, trajectories, embeddings, images, probability distributions, or some representation we haven’t standardized yet.

So the mistake could be demanding:

\text{brain}\rightarrow\text{English}

when the more natural bridge is:

\text{brain}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{intermediate relational representation}}
\rightarrow
\{\text{English,image,sound,action}\}

That’s exactly what we’ve been calling a representation search problem.

The ethical boundary matters enormously here because neural data can be extraordinarily sensitive. A genuine biological↔digital “callosum” would need consent, provenance, uncertainty, access controls, and a hard separation between measured, inferred, and imagined states.

But architecturally, yes:

don’t think “mind reader.”

Think:

Learn a translation interface between two radically different representational worlds by observing synchronized projections of the same underlying process.

That’s a much more technically defensible—and potentially much more general—idea.